item.content:encoded:
I run a small daily game, Costcodle, where everyone guesses the price of the same Costco item each day. Just after midnight UTC, a scheduled job publishes the answer page of every puzzle that has finished. It is a tiny job, and for a while it was the part of the system I trusted least.The reason was a migration. I was merging two Cloudflare Workers, one for the website and one for scheduled jobs, into a single Worker. Until the old one was deleted, both would fire the same cron string at the same moment, against the same database. I wanted three guarantees to survive that overlap:Every answer page is published exactly once, and the site's publication revision goes up exactly once per page.Once a page is published, no edge location serves the old version again.None of this depends on purging a cache.The database is Cloudflare D1, and that is where it gets interesting.Why D1 Makes "Exactly Once" HarderD1 gives you . The documentation is clear about what it does: "Batched statements are SQL transactions," and if a statement in the sequence fails, D1 "aborts or rolls back the entire sequence" (D1 Database API).That is a strong guarantee about errors. It says nothing about statements that succeed and do nothing. An  whose  clause matches zero rows is not an error, so the batch carries on and commits whatever the other statements did.What the Worker binding does not give you is an interactive transaction: open it, read a row, decide in your own code, then write, all under one lock. So the natural pattern, "check that I still hold the lease, then write," has a gap in it. Between the read that checks and the batch that writes, another job can take over. If the batch then runs three statements and only some of their conditions still hold, you get exactly the state you were trying to avoid: an item marked published with no page, or a page with no revision bump.The fix I ended up with has two parts. A lease with a fencing token decides who may publish. A guard repeated inside every statement of the final batch makes sure a writer that lost the lease changes nothing at all.Leases With Fencing Tokens, in a TableLeases live in a  table: a key such as , an owner id, an expiry and a  counter. Taking a lease is a single upsert (simplified from the real repository):const rows = await db.insert(jobLeases).values({
  key, ownerId, fence: 1, expiresAt, updatedAt: now,
}).onConflictDoUpdate({
  target: jobLeases.key,
  set: { ownerId, fence: sql`${jobLeases.fence} + 1`, expiresAt, updatedAt: now },
  // Only an expired lease can change hands.
  setWhere: sql`${jobLeases.expiresAt} <= ${now}`,
}).returning();

return rows[0] ?? null; // null: someone else holds it
If nobody holds the key, the insert creates it with fence 1. If the current lease has expired, the conflict branch hands it to the new owner and increments the fence. If the lease is still live, the  clause matches nothing, no row comes back, and the caller skips that item for now. Each run gets a fresh random owner id, so two Workers firing the same cron look like two independent owners competing for the same keys. My leases last 180 seconds.In my case the storage layer is the same SQLite database that holds the lease, which makes that check unusually cheap. It can be part of the write.A Guard in Every StatementEvery write that depends on the lease carries this condition:EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM job_leases
        WHERE key = ? AND owner_id = ? AND fence = ? AND expires_at > ?)
If another job has taken over, the fence no longer matches, and the condition is false inside the very statement that would have written.The final publish batch takes this further. Its first statement inserts or updates the page with INSERT ... SELECT ... WHERE <guard>, and the guard re-checks everything the job verified earlier, in SQL: the lease, the ledger item's state, the question bank version, that the image is ready, and that the page is still at the content version the job expected (plain optimistic concurrency). That statement also writes a random . Every statement after it is conditioned on that token having landed:// Simplified from the publish commit.
const token = crypto.randomUUID();
const changed = sql`EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM answer_posts
  WHERE puzzle_id = ${id} AND commit_token = ${token})`;

const result = await db.batch([
  // 1. The page itself: written only when the guard holds.
  db.insert(answerPosts).select(sql`SELECT ${id}, ..., ${token} WHERE ${guard}`)
    .onConflictDoUpdate({ target: answerPosts.puzzleId, set: { ...page, commitToken: token },
      setWhere: sql`${answerPosts.contentVersion} = ${expectedVersion}` }),
  // 2. The ledger item, only if statement 1 wrote this token.
  db.update(publicationItems).set({ state: "published" })
    .where(and(eq(publicationItems.puzzleId, id), changed)),
  // 3. The site revision, under the same condition.
  db.update(siteState).set({ publicationRevision: sql`${siteState.publicationRevision} + 1` })
    .where(and(eq(siteState.id, 1), changed)),
  // 4. Read back what happened.
  db.select().from(answerPosts).where(eq(answerPosts.puzzleId, id)),
]);
If the guard is false, statement 1 writes nothing, so the token never lands, so statements 2 and 3 match zero rows as well. The zero propagates through the whole batch. The job reads the page back, sees a different token, and reports  or  without having changed anything.The same guard enforces two one-way rules I care about. A page that search engines may index never goes back to noindex, and a page that has its product photo never loses it. Both are expressed as conditions on the existing row, so a stale or confused writer cannot undo them.There is a test for the exact gap described earlier. It lets the job finish its read batch, then has a second repository take over the lease before the final batch runs. The call returns , and the test then checks the database: no page row, a publication revision still at 0, and the ledger item still marked as processing.A Revision Number Instead of a PurgeStatement 3 above is the second half of the design. Every change to published content, a new page, an updated page or a withdrawal, bumps one global  in the same atomic batch that made the change. A rerun that changes nothing public leaves it alone.The edge cache uses that number as part of every key for content that depends on publications:export function cacheKey(scope: CacheScope, ...parts: (string | number)[]): Request {
  const path = [scope.environment, scope.origin, scope.buildVersion, `c${CONTRACT_VERSION}`, ...parts]
    .map((part) => encodeURIComponent(String(part))).join("/");
  return new Request(`https://cache.costcodle.internal/${path}`);
}

// For an answer page: cacheKey(scope, utcDay, "content", `r${revision}`, "/answers/1121")
Each request reads the current revision from the primary database first and only then looks in the cache. After a publication, every request builds a key that has never been stored, misses, renders the fresh page and stores it under the new key. The old entries are still there. Nothing will ever ask for them again, and they age out on their own.This matters more on Workers than it might elsewhere. The Cache API documentation notes that "the contents of the cache do not replicate outside of the originating data center," and that  "only purges content of the cache in the data center that the Worker was invoked." A purge from inside a Worker is a local operation. Changing the key is global by construction.A few rules keep this honest:Browsers and shared caches only ever see Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate. The longer lifetime applies to the internal Cache API copy.That internal copy lives at most 300 seconds for content pages and 60 seconds for the home page.If the revision cannot be read, the cache layer steps aside and the page reports the failure itself. A database fault never turns into a stale 200.The general pattern of versioned cache keys is covered well in The Many Layers of Caching. What surprised me was the one case it did not cover.The change that is not a publicationIn my game, yesterday's puzzle stays playable for a 10 minute grace window after midnight UTC, and its answer must not become public until that window ends. At 00:10 UTC, yesterday's answer becomes publishable. No publication has happened at that instant, so the revision has not changed.If a "not published yet" response had been cached at 00:09 with a 300 second lifetime, it would keep answering until 00:14 under a perfectly valid key. So content entries get a lifetime of the smaller of 300 seconds and the time left until 00:10 UTC. If your data has any moment where visibility changes without a write, a revision-keyed cache needs the same treatment: find that moment and make sure no entry lives across it.The price of the whole scheme is one database read per cacheable request, to learn the revision. For a site of this size that is a good trade.The Same Idea in One StatementThe pattern of putting every condition inside the write also works without a lease. After each game, the client can report the result for the day's shared stats. The rules are: one row per game session, at most 50,000 rows per puzzle, and a dense slot number for each row. All three are handled by one statement:INSERT INTO daily_results (puzzle_id, session_id, slot, outcome, guesses_used, created_at)
SELECT ?, ?, next.slot, ?, ?, ?
FROM (SELECT coalesce((SELECT max(slot) FROM daily_results WHERE puzzle_id = ?), 0) + 1 AS slot) AS next
WHERE next.slot <= 50000
ON CONFLICT (puzzle_id, session_id) DO NOTHING
SQLite runs a single statement atomically and D1 serializes writes to a database, so two concurrent reports cannot take the same slot, a duplicate session inserts nothing, and a full puzzle inserts nothing. There is no window between the check and the write because there is no separate check.One detail took a test to get right. The bare  sits alone in its own scalar subquery. SQLite's MIN/MAX optimization can answer a single  over the leading column of an index "by doing a single index lookup rather than by scanning the entire table," and wrapping it in a larger expression loses that. A test seeds 49,999 rows for one puzzle and asserts that the next insert reads fewer than 20 rows. For the  clauses used throughout, the SQLite UPSERT documentation is the reference.Proving It on Real InfrastructureUnit tests cover each piece: concurrent lease claims, two concurrent compare-and-set commits where only one wins, and the takeover between preflight and commit. The test I trust most runs on the real stack. An integration run starts the actual Worker with D1 and R2 and fires the publish cron twice at the same instant, the way the old and new Workers would during the merge. Then it fires the hourly compensation job twice at once, several rounds in a row.The result: one daily quota row, six pages each published exactly once at content version 1, a publication revision of exactly 6, and seven source images each downloaded and stored exactly once. Two more simultaneous compensation runs afterwards find nothing to do and change nothing.What I Would Reuse AnywherePut the condition inside the write. "Check, then write" across two round trips is a race, even with a lease.Chain a batch with a token. When your atomic unit only rolls back on errors, make every later statement depend on a value only the first statement could have written. A zero then propagates through the batch.Let the storage check the fence. A fencing token that only the client looks at protects nothing.Version the key instead of purging. A monotonically increasing revision, bumped in the same transaction as the content, makes invalidation instant everywhere and needs no purge API.Hunt for changes that are not writes. Time-based visibility, embargoes and grace windows change what a URL should return without touching the revision. Cap cache lifetimes at those instants.How do you get exactly-once behavior out of a store that only offers batches? I would like to hear what other people have built on D1 or plain SQLite.Costcodle is an independent fan game. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored by Costco Wholesale Corporation.

is not a resilience strategy. It is a retry loop that hides an ordering bug until the night it doesn't. A Compose stack that survives production treats startup order, network reachability, credentials, and resource ceilings as explicit, testable contracts. This piece takes a typical four-service stack (Nginx, a FastAPI app, PostgreSQL 16, Redis 7) and hardens each of those contracts in turn.1. The Fragility of Naive StacksMost Compose files start life like this:services: api: build: ./api ports: ["8000:8000"] depends_on: [postgres, redis] postgres: image: postgres:16-alpine environment: POSTGRES_PASSWORD: devpassword redis: image: redis:7-alpine ports: ["6379:6379"] It works on a laptop and fails in four predictable ways elsewhere: On an empty volume, the Postgres entrypoint runs , starts a temporary server, applies init scripts, stops it, and starts the real server. That takes seconds. Short-form only waits for the container to , so the API's first connection attempt lands mid-initialization and fails. If the API runs migrations at boot and you scale to two replicas, two processes race to apply the same schema change. If you run migrations by hand, the app can serve traffic against an old schema. Every service shares the default network, and publishes Redis on every host interface. Anything that reaches the host reaches the cache, and anything that compromises the proxy can reach the database directly. sits in the Compose file, in output, and in the environment of every process in the container.2. Deterministic Startup via Health ChecksCompose v2 implements the Compose Specification's long-form , where each dependency carries a condition:The dependency's container has started (the default)Dependencies with their own connection retry logicThe dependency's reports Databases, caches, upstream APIsservice_completed_successfullyThe dependency ran to completion and exited Migrations, seed jobs, one-shot init tasksThe distinction that matters is process versus readiness. tells you exists. It says nothing about whether port 5432 accepts connections. gates on a probe you define:healthcheck: test: ["CMD-SHELL", "pg_isready -h 127.0.0.1 -U app -d app"] interval: 5s # time between probes timeout: 3s # a probe slower than this counts as a failure retries: 10 # consecutive failures before "unhealthy" start_period: 30s # failures here don't count toward retries Note the . During first-boot initialization, the official Postgres entrypoint runs its temporary server with : Unix socket only. A socket-based can report ready init, just before that server is stopped. Probing over TCP only succeeds once the real server is listening.Migrations become their own service. It runs once, after the database is healthy, and the application gates on its exit code:migrate: command: ["alembic", "upgrade", "head"] depends_on: postgres: condition: service_healthy restart: "no"
app: depends_on: migrate: condition: service_completed_successfully If a migration fails, the app never starts, which is exactly what you want. And because only one container applies the schema, scaling no longer causes races.3. Network Segmentation & Secrets Hardening Internet │ Host :80 (:443 when TLS terminates here) │ ┌──────────────┼───────────────────── dmz ─────────────┐ │ ▼ │ │ ┌─────────────────┐ proxy_pass ┌────────────┐ │ │ │ Nginx (UID 101) │ ───────────▶ │ FastAPI │ │ │ └─────────────────┘ :8000 │ (UID 10001)│ │ │ └─────┬──────┘ │ └────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────┘ ┌────────────────────────── backend_net ─────┼─────────┐ │ (internal: true, no egress) │ │ │ ┌──────────────────┴──────┐ │ │ ▼ ▼ │ │ ┌──────────────────┐ ┌──────────────┐│ │ │ PostgreSQL 16 │ │ Redis 7 ││ │ │ (UID 70) :5432 │ │ (UID 999) ││ │ └──────────────────┘ └──────────────┘│ │ no host ports; unreachable from nginx │ └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ Split the topology into two user-defined bridge networks:: Nginx and the app. Only Nginx publishes a host port.: the app, Postgres, and Redis, marked . Docker creates no route from an internal network to the outside, so the data stores cannot reach the internet and publish nothing to the host.The app is the only service on both networks. Nginx cannot resolve the name , because it shares no network with it.For credentials, move passwords out of into top-level . Compose mounts each secret at , and the value never appears in or the process environment. The official Postgres image reads variants natively:environment: POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE: /run/secrets/db_password Your own code reads the file the same way:import os from pathlib import Path
def read_secret(env_var: str) -> str: return Path(os.environ[env_var]).read_text().strip()
db_password = read_secret("DB_PASSWORD_FILE") One caveat: outside Swarm, Compose implements file-sourced secrets as bind mounts, and silently ignores the long-syntax , , and options for them. They only apply to secrets sourced from . With file secrets, the host file's permissions decide who inside the container can read it. Lock down the on the host () and make the files readable by the service UIDs (). A container reads a bind-mounted file without traversing the host directory.4. Resource Limits & Non-Root EnforcementWithout limits, one leaking worker can trigger the kernel OOM killer for the whole host, taking down the database with it. Compose v2 applies outside Swarm:deploy: resources: limits: cpus: "1.0" # hard CFS quota: at most one core of CPU time memory: 512M # cgroup ceiling; exceeding it kills this container, not its neighbors pids: 200 # caps fork bombs and thread leaks reservations: memory: 256M is a ceiling. The separate key is only a relative weight that applies when CPUs are contended. Use limits for isolation, and shares only to prioritize. runs as a fixed non-root UID that matches no account on the host, so a container escape lands on an identity that owns nothing. makes the root filesystem immutable; give each service writable paths only where it needs scratch space. and security_opt: ["no-new-privileges:true"] remove capabilities and block setuid escalation.Use image-native non-root UIDs where they exist: 70 for , 999:1000 for , and 101 for nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged. Running the images as those users from the start means the entrypoints never need root to drop privileges.Volume ownership is the trap here. A volume is safe: Docker seeds it from the image's , which the Postgres image creates with permissions that accept a fixed . A of a host directory is not. The directory is typically owned by root, running as UID 70 cannot write to it, and the container exits on first boot. If you must bind-mount, the host path first. Never reuse a data directory initialized by the Debian-based image, whose user is UID 999.5. The Production Blueprintname: fastapi-platform
# Shared hardening, merged into every service x-hardening: &hardening read_only: true cap_drop: [ALL] security_opt: - no-new-privileges:true restart: unless-stopped
x-api-image: &api-image build: context: ./api image: fastapi-platform/api:1.0.0
services: nginx: <<: *hardening image: nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged:1.27-alpine user: "101:101" ports: - "80:8080" # the only published port in the stack volumes: - ./nginx/default.conf:/etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf:ro tmpfs: - /tmp:size=16m # pid file and proxy buffers networks: [dmz] depends_on: app: condition: service_healthy deploy: resources: limits: { cpus: "0.50", memory: 128M, pids: 100 }
app: <<: [*hardening, *api-image] user: "10001:10001" environment: DB_HOST: postgres DB_NAME: app DB_USER: app DB_PASSWORD_FILE: /run/secrets/db_password REDIS_HOST: redis REDIS_PASSWORD_FILE: /run/secrets/redis_password secrets: [db_password, redis_password] tmpfs: - /tmp:size=64m networks: [dmz, backend_net] # no ports: reachable only through nginx depends_on: migrate: condition: service_completed_successfully redis: condition: service_healthy healthcheck: test: ["CMD", "python", "-c", "import urllib.request; urllib.request.urlopen('http://127.0.0.1:8000/healthz', timeout=2)"] interval: 10s timeout: 3s retries: 3 start_period: 20s deploy: resources: limits: { cpus: "1.0", memory: 512M, pids: 200 } reservations: { memory: 256M }
migrate: <<: [*hardening, *api-image] user: "10001:10001" command: ["alembic", "upgrade", "head"] restart: "no" # one-shot: overrides the shared policy environment: DB_HOST: postgres DB_NAME: app DB_USER: app DB_PASSWORD_FILE: /run/secrets/db_password secrets: [db_password] tmpfs: - /tmp:size=16m networks: [backend_net] depends_on: postgres: condition: service_healthy
postgres: <<: *hardening image: postgres:16-alpine user: "70:70" # image-native postgres UID; no root entrypoint step environment: POSTGRES_DB: app POSTGRES_USER: app POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE: /run/secrets/db_password secrets: [db_password] volumes: - pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data tmpfs: - /var/run/postgresql:size=1m # Unix socket and lock file - /tmp:size=16m shm_size: 128mb networks: [backend_net] healthcheck: test: ["CMD-SHELL", "pg_isready -h 127.0.0.1 -U app -d app"] interval: 5s timeout: 3s retries: 10 start_period: 30s stop_grace_period: 60s # time to checkpoint before SIGKILL deploy: resources: limits: { cpus: "2.0", memory: 1G, pids: 300 }
redis: <<: *hardening image: redis:7-alpine user: "999:1000" # The password arrives on stdin ("-"), so it never appears in the process list. # $$ escapes Compose interpolation so the shell evaluates $(cat ...). command: - sh - -c - | exec redis-server - \ --maxmemory 192mb \ --maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru \ --save "" \ --appendonly no <<EOF requirepass $$(cat /run/secrets/redis_password) EOF secrets: [redis_password] networks: [backend_net] healthcheck: test: ["CMD-SHELL", "REDISCLI_AUTH=\"$$(cat /run/secrets/redis_password)\" redis-cli ping | grep -q PONG"] interval: 5s timeout: 3s retries: 5 start_period: 5s deploy: resources: limits: { cpus: "0.50", memory: 256M, pids: 100 }
networks: dmz: driver: bridge backend_net: driver: bridge internal: true # no route to the outside world
volumes: pgdata:
secrets: db_password: file: ./secrets/db_password.txt redis_password: file: ./secrets/redis_password.txt Redis runs as a pure cache: persistence is off, and sits at 75% of the container's memory limit. That headroom covers connection buffers and fragmentation, so Redis evicts keys before the cgroup kills it.Nginx rate-limits per client IP before traffic reaches the app:# nginx/default.conf (included in the http context) limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=api:10m rate=20r/s; limit_req_status 429;
server { listen 8080; server_tokens off; client_max_body_size 1m;
location / { limit_req zone=api burst=40 nodelay; proxy_pass http://app:8000; proxy_set_header Host $host; proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr; proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for; proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme; proxy_connect_timeout 2s; proxy_read_timeout 30s; } } If Nginx sits behind a cloud load balancer, configure with the load balancer's range. Otherwise is the load balancer's IP, and every client shares a single rate-limit bucket. TLS terminates either at that load balancer or in an additional server block. On rootless Docker, publishing host port 80 or 443 fails unless net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start is lowered, so map to unprivileged host ports there and front them with the load balancer.migrate exited 0, redis healthy6. Operational Verification & Open Source ReferenceHardening that isn't verified is a guess. After docker compose up -d --wait, which blocks until services are running and healthy:# Validate the merged model: anchors, interpolation, secrets paths docker compose config --quiet
# Health per service: expect "healthy" for app, postgres and redis # (nginx has no healthcheck; it starts only after app is healthy) docker compose ps
# The migration must have exited 0 docker compose ps -a migrate --format '{{.Service}} {{.State}} exit={{.ExitCode}}'
# Full probe history when something stays "starting" docker inspect --format '{{json .State.Health}}' "$(docker compose ps -q postgres)"
# Non-root and immutable: expect uid=10001 and "Read-only file system" docker compose exec app sh -c 'id; touch /probe'
# Segmentation: nginx must not resolve the database docker compose exec nginx getent hosts postgres || echo "isolated"
# Limits applied: the LIMIT column should match deploy.resources docker stats --no-stream Wire the first three checks into CI, so a regression in startup ordering fails the pipeline instead of a deploy.Run the blueprint yourselfThe complete, runnable topology lives in the production-compose/ directory of devops-infra-toolkit. It contains this exact , the Nginx configuration, and a minimal FastAPI service with an Alembic migration. Generate the two secret files, run docker compose up --build --wait, and every check above should pass. The repository's top-level is the naive shape from section 1, so diffing the two files shows every control in this piece side by side.Compose hardening contains what runs inside the stack. It does not govern who may drive the Docker daemon itself. That boundary matters most when the client is an autonomous AI agent. The security architecture specification of docker-mcp-guardian lays out that container isolation model. It covers why socket access is root-equivalent, how a read-only-by-default MCP layer narrows what an agent can ask of the daemon, and where a filtering socket proxy fits. Issues and pull requests are welcome on both repositories.
